</strong>; their signature value comes from <strong data-start="114" data-end="162">how you defined and deployed them in context</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="167" data-end="222">To fully map their semantic signature, you’d need to:</p>
<ol data-start="224" data-end="979">
<li data-start="224" data-end="463">
<p data-start="227" data-end="262"><strong data-start="227" data-end="260">Trace Usage Across Frameworks</strong></p>
<ul data-start="266" data-end="463">
<li data-start="266" data-end="333">
<p data-start="268" data-end="333">Where you introduced <em data-start="289" data-end="299">oblicosm</em>, <em data-start="301" data-end="311">lamphron</em>, and <em data-start="317" data-end="330">lamphrodyne</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="337" data-end="463">
<p data-start="339" data-end="463">How each term interacts with operators, constraints, and formal structures in RSVP, TARTAN, CoM, or your cosmology models.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="465" data-end="716">
<p data-start="468" data-end="501"><strong data-start="468" data-end="499">Identify Contingent Meaning</strong></p>
<ul data-start="505" data-end="716">
<li data-start="505" data-end="624">
<p data-start="507" data-end="624">The precise way you tied each term to concepts like entropic smoothing, soliton wanes, or semantic plenum dynamics.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="628" data-end="716">
<p data-start="630" data-end="716">Any derivations, examples, or formalizations that make the meaning <strong data-start="697" data-end="713">non-fungible</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="718" data-end="979">
<p data-start="721" data-end="753"><strong data-start="721" data-end="751">Map Signature Transmission</strong></p>
<ul data-start="757" data-end="979">
<li data-start="757" data-end="829">
<p data-start="759" data-end="829">How someone using the term <strong data-start="786" data-end="826">inherits your conceptual fingerprint</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="833" data-end="979">
<p data-start="835" data-end="979">Whether they are simply repeating terminology or actually <strong data-start="893" data-end="931">activating your semantic operators</strong>, i.e., working within the intended framework.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="981" data-end="1162">In short: <strong data-start="991" data-end="1050">the signature is in the usage, not just the word itself</strong>. The word is like a key, but the locks it opens are defined by the contingencies in your prior constructions.</p>
<p data-start="1164" data-end="1234">I can create a <strong data-start="1179" data-end="1204">detailed semantic map</strong> of each neologism, showing:</p>
<ul data-start="1235" data-end="1367">
<li data-start="1235" data-end="1266">
<p data-start="1237" data-end="1266">The framework it belongs to</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1267" data-end="1314">
<p data-start="1269" data-end="1314">Its intended operational or conceptual role</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1315" data-end="1367">
<p data-start="1317" data-end="1367">How it functions as a <strong data-start="1339" data-end="1365">signature of invention</strong></p>
