<ul data-start="2912" data-end="3058">
<li data-start="2912" data-end="2961">
<p data-start="2914" data-end="2961">A change in text or workflow changes <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">F</span></span></span></span>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2962" data-end="3058">
<p data-start="2964" data-end="3058">Different users can share K to replicate interpretation, but F remains tied to both A and K.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3060" data-end="3063">
<p data-start="3065" data-end="3234">Essentially, this makes <strong data-start="3089" data-end="3135">every work a non-fungible epistemic object</strong>: it’s unique not only by content but also by the interpretive machinery needed to understand it.</p>
<hr data-start="3236" data-end="3239">
<p data-start="3241" data-end="3486">If you want, I can <strong data-start="3260" data-end="3310">draw a small conceptual “diagram” in text form</strong> showing <strong data-start="3319" data-end="3363">artifact → hash → pipeline → fingerprint</strong> with examples of neologisms, workflows, and frameworks. This makes the system immediately visualizable without graphics.</p>
