iv class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="50">Here’s a <strong data-start="9" data-end="47">summary of the conversation so far</strong>:</p>
<hr data-start="52" data-end="55">
<ol data-start="57" data-end="2368">
<li data-start="57" data-end="345">
<p data-start="60" data-end="93"><strong data-start="60" data-end="91">NFT / cryptographic framing</strong></p>
<ul data-start="97" data-end="345">
<li data-start="97" data-end="203">
<p data-start="99" data-end="203">You initially explored the idea of identifying creative works—art, music, text—as non-fungible tokens.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="207" data-end="345">
<p data-start="209" data-end="345">You clarified that you didn’t mean literal NFTs, but rather cryptographic or steganographic signatures that uniquely identify artifacts.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="347" data-end="821">
<p data-start="350" data-end="392"><strong data-start="350" data-end="390">Identification via style and context</strong></p>
<ul data-start="396" data-end="821">
<li data-start="396" data-end="537">
<p data-start="398" data-end="537">We discussed how unique word usage, neologisms, workflows, and deviations from standard methods can serve as identifiers or fingerprints.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="541" data-end="697">
<p data-start="543" data-end="697">Your habits (like shell scripts, draft directory structures, or parsing pipelines) are part of the artifact’s “context,” contributing to its uniqueness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="701" data-end="821">
<p data-start="703" data-end="821">Even the way artifacts are consumed—via public keys or parsing pipelines—acts as part of their non-fungible signature.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="823" data-end="1491">
<p data-start="826" data-end="877"><strong data-start="826" data-end="875">Category- and sheaf-theoretic interpretations</strong></p>
<ul data-start="881" data-end="1491">
<li data-start="881" data-end="1064">
<p data-start="883" data-end="1064">In sheaf-theoretic terms: each artifact is a section over a context (the site being the workspace or project), and morphisms (restrictions) correspond to access or interpretation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1068" data-end="1491">
<p data-start="1070" data-end="1100">In category-theoretic terms:</p>
<ul data-start="1106" data-end="1491">
<li data-start="1106" data-end="1142">
<p data-start="1108" data-end="1142">Artifacts + context are objects.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1148" data-end="1203">
<p data-start="1150" data-end="1203">Viewing methods or parsing pipelines are morphisms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1209" data-end="1281">
<p data-start="1211" data-end="1281">Identity morphisms correspond to full native-context interpretation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1287" data-end="1383">
<p data-start="1289" data-end="1383">Functors can represent merging or combining artifacts while preserving context dependencies.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1389" data-end="1491">
<p data-start="1391" data-end="1491">Sparse heuristics align with partial morphisms, allowing efficient, context-sensitive understanding.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1493" data-end="1759">
<p data-start="1496" data-end="1540"><strong data-start="1496" data-end="1538">Non-fungibility and context-dependence</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1544" data-end="1759">
<li data-start="1544" data-end="1657">
<p data-start="1546" data-end="1657">Two artifacts with the same content can be distinct if their workflows, scripts, or parsing pipelines differ.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1661" data-end="1759">
<p data-start="1663" data-end="1759">Hashes combined with public keys (or other prerequisite knowledge) formalize this distinction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1761" data-end="2051">
<p data-start="1764" data-end="1805"><strong data-start="1764" data-end="1803">Sparse and efficient interpretation</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1809" data-end="2051">
<li data-start="1809" data-end="1943">
<p data-start="1811" data-end="1943">Sparse heuristics / GBSH ideas apply: inference doesn’t require exhaustive information but can rely on selected, salient pathways.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1947" data-end="2051">
<p data-start="1949" data-end="2051">This connects back to RSVP theory and the entropy-aware, context-dependent field of interpretations.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="2053" data-end="2368">
<p data-start="2056" data-end="2086"><strong data-start="2056" data-end="2084">Plain-language synthesis</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2090" data-end="2368">
<li data-start="2090" data-end="2269">
<p data-start="2092" data-end="2269">We distilled the formalism into a more accessible explanation: artifacts are uniquely defined by content + context, and morphisms capture how they are accessed or interpreted.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2273" data-end="2368">
<p data-start="2275" data-end="2368">Non-fungibility emerges naturally from differences in context, workflow, and access method.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="2370" data-end="2373">
<p data-start="2375" data-end="2728">In short: <strong data-start="2385" data-end="2726">Your artifacts are non-fungible because their identity depends not just on content, but also on context, workflow, and the pipelines used to view or interpret them. This can be formalized categorically, with objects as artifacts and morphisms as interpretation methods, while sparse heuristics capture efficient, selective understanding.</strong></p>
