ed so it naturally transitions into RSVP analysis:</p>
<hr data-start="174" data-end="177">
<h3 data-start="179" data-end="238"><strong data-start="183" data-end="238">I. Criticisms of Big Tech and Chokepoint Capitalism</strong></h3>
<p data-start="240" data-end="877">In recent decades, the rise of global technology platforms has generated unprecedented efficiency and connectivity, but it has also concentrated power in the hands of a few corporations, creating <strong data-start="436" data-end="451">chokepoints</strong> in the digital infrastructure. Critics of big tech argue that these chokepoints manifest in multiple, often contradictory ways: platforms that claim to democratize information simultaneously control the dissemination and monetization of content; services designed for openness often impose opaque algorithms that reinforce centralization; and corporate rhetoric of innovation frequently masks extractive economic practices.</p>
<p data-start="879" data-end="1464">These contradictions are not merely rhetorical—they reflect a structural tension between <strong data-start="968" data-end="986">local autonomy</strong> and <strong data-start="991" data-end="1009">global control</strong>. Individuals and communities may attempt to preserve local coherence, producing content, tools, or alternative platforms, yet these efforts are invariably entangled with global infrastructure and economic networks. For instance, open-source developers rely on proprietary hosting services; digital activists critique monopolies while using dominant communication protocols; and creators seeking freedom must often navigate the very systems they resist.</p>
<p data-start="1466" data-end="2004">Viewed through a systemic lens, these criticisms reveal an inherent <strong data-start="1534" data-end="1553">phase imbalance</strong>: local actors generate coherent, low-entropy contributions—innovative projects, ethical discourse, and alternative workflows—but global flows of capital, attention, and control disperse this coherence, restoring the system’s overall entropy. In other words, <strong data-start="1812" data-end="1888">the very act of critique is coupled to the mechanisms it seeks to oppose</strong>, making contradictions a predictable feature of chokepoint capitalism rather than a failure of individual intent.</p>
<p data-start="2006" data-end="2578">This interplay between local and global constraints forms a natural segue into the <strong data-start="2089" data-end="2107">RSVP framework</strong>, which provides a formal vocabulary for analyzing such paradoxes. In RSVP terms, local coherence corresponds to <strong data-start="2220" data-end="2241">lamphron clusters</strong>, while the dispersive influence of large-scale infrastructure is mediated by <strong data-start="2319" data-end="2340">lamphrodyne flows</strong>. Understanding these dynamics allows us to reinterpret contradictions not as flaws, but as <strong data-start="2432" data-end="2491">thermodynamic invariants of semantic and social systems</strong>—the signature patterns through which critique, creativity, and resistance propagate.</p>
<hr data-start="2580" data-end="2583">
<p data-start="2585" data-end="2832">If you want, the next section can <strong data-start="2619" data-end="2690">map these contradictions explicitly into the RSVP fields (Φ, 𝒗, S)</strong> and illustrate how semantic modules and lamphron–lamphrodyne flows formalize the tension between critique and infrastructural entanglement.</p>
<p data-start="2834" data-end="2884" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to continue with that mapping next?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16